<DocsLayout title="Files and assets" activeNav="/platform/files-and-assets">
  <h1>Files and assets</h1>
  <p>
    An application's media library holds two different things. <strong>Files</strong> are arbitrary
    attachments with metadata — mapping files, release checklists, provisioning notes.
    <strong>App assets</strong> are the icon and splash screen, from which the platform generates
    every per-platform size you need at build time.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="files-and-assets"
    alt="Files and Media Library tab showing two attached files as cards with metadata, download and delete actions"
    caption="Files and App assets are separate tabs with separate rules."
  />

  <h2>Files</h2>
  <p>
    Any file type, up to 100 MB each. Each card shows the extension, size, upload time, and three
    actions: inspect <strong>Metadata</strong>, download, delete.
  </p>
  <p>
    Files are addressed <strong>by name</strong>, not by id — in the URL, in the API, and in the
    CLI. Two consequences follow:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Uploading a name that already exists does not overwrite the existing file. The platform
      de-duplicates by giving the new upload a distinct name. Delete the old one first if you meant
      to replace it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Names cannot contain slashes. That restriction is what keeps a name from escaping the app's
      own collection.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Metadata</h3>
  <p>
    Metadata is a free-form JSON object attached to a file. It is the practical way to record where
    a file came from, and it is what makes an old file identifiable a year later:
  </p>
  <pre><code>&#123;"build_id": "019fc789-47b5-7034-9291-4cd3c57b75d5", "version": "1.4.0"&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>
    Saving replaces the whole object rather than merging into it, so send every key you want to
    keep.
  </p>

  <h3>What belongs here, and what does not</h3>
  <p>
    Files belong to the <em>application</em> and outlive any single build. Build outputs belong to
    the build that produced them, as <a href="/platform/builds">artifacts</a>, and disappear with
    it. The usual split in a pipeline: the AAB or IPA goes to artifacts, the symbol mapping goes
    here, where it stays findable after the build ages out.
  </p>

  <h2>App assets</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>App assets</strong> tab holds one icon and one splash screen. Uploading replaces
    what is there — there is no version history — and both must be images.
  </p>
  <p>
    From the original, the platform generates every platform-specific size: iOS icon sizes from 40
    to 1024 points, Android density buckets from mdpi to xxxhdpi plus the Play Store icon, and
    splash screens for the common iOS and Android resolutions. Each generated variant is
    downloadable on its own, so a build script can fetch exactly the size it needs instead of
    resizing at build time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Generation runs after upload rather than during it, so a variant list can briefly show fewer
    entries than it will a moment later. A variant that never appears has not been generated, and
    requesting it answers <code>404</code> rather than silently falling back to the original.
  </p>
  <p>
    Replacing an icon also triggers an Expo sync when the app is linked to an Expo project, so the
    two sides do not drift — see <a href="/platform/integrations/eas">EAS</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Doing the same from the CLI</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="/guides/cli/files"><code>appmctl files</code></a> — list, inspect, download, upload,
      update metadata, delete
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/guides/cli/applications"><code>appmctl assets</code></a> — list every generated
      variant with its download URL, then fetch one
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>appmctl assets list</code> is the quickest way to see which variants actually exist for an
    app, which is more reliable than assuming a size was generated.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
